WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.585
Welcome to Day 2 of Case Management Training with Legal Support Help.

00:00:04.935 --> 00:00:08.395
Yesterday we carried a case from intake through the Treatment Phase.

00:00:08.745 --> 00:00:12.758
Today we follow it to the money: how we audit the file before the demand,

00:00:12.758 --> 00:00:15.341
how we build and send the Bodily Injury demand,

00:00:15.341 --> 00:00:17.870
and how we negotiate and close the settlement.

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:23.189
Keep John Doe's file in mind as we go, because every phase today comes back to it.

00:00:25.739 --> 00:00:28.444
Here is our agenda for today, in three parts.

00:00:28.794 --> 00:00:33.506
Part one: the Pre-Demand phase, where we audit the whole file before anything goes

00:00:33.506 --> 00:00:34.425
to the adjuster.

00:00:34.775 --> 00:00:39.563
Part two: the Bodily Injury Demand, where we build the demand packet and present the

00:00:39.563 --> 00:00:39.848
case.

00:00:40.198 --> 00:00:43.291
Part three: the BI Settlement, where we negotiate,

00:00:43.291 --> 00:00:47.188
finalize the paperwork, and move the file from settled to paid.

00:00:49.738 --> 00:00:54.239
Pre-demand case auditing, often called a Forensic Case Audit or a Pre-Litigation

00:00:54.239 --> 00:00:58.684
Audit, is the rigorous process of vetting a claim's evidence and value before a

00:00:58.684 --> 00:01:00.372
formal demand letter goes out.

00:01:00.722 --> 00:01:02.754
It has four core objectives.

00:01:03.104 --> 00:01:07.125
Verification: do the facts in the demand letter match the physical evidence,

00:01:07.125 --> 00:01:10.511
for example, do the photos of the crash match the police report?

00:01:10.861 --> 00:01:13.935
Valuation: are the damages calculated accurately,

00:01:13.935 --> 00:01:19.078
with the medical bills, lost wages, and future care costs audited so every expense

00:01:19.078 --> 00:01:19.894
is justified?

00:01:20.244 --> 00:01:24.655
Risk assessment: where are the weak spots where the defense may argue comparative

00:01:24.655 --> 00:01:27.651
negligence, meaning partial fault on the client's part?

00:01:28.001 --> 00:01:31.402
And compliance: does the case meet statutory requirements,

00:01:31.402 --> 00:01:34.978
like the Statute of Limitations, before the demand is issued?

00:01:35.328 --> 00:01:38.614
The audit is a sanity check that makes the claim bulletproof.

00:01:41.164 --> 00:01:45.364
The goal of this audit is simple: when the demand letter lands on an adjuster's

00:01:45.364 --> 00:01:47.863
desk, there are zero easy outs for the defense.

00:01:48.213 --> 00:01:51.940
A successful audit keeps three pillars perfectly synchronized.

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:55.587
The Narrative: the client's story of how the incident happened.

00:01:55.937 --> 00:01:59.204
The Physicality: the property damage, and the biomechanics,

00:01:59.204 --> 00:02:00.976
meaning the force of the impact.

00:02:01.326 --> 00:02:03.949
And the Medicals: the clinical proof of injury.

00:02:04.299 --> 00:02:07.643
If one pillar is out of alignment, the case value collapses.

00:02:07.993 --> 00:02:12.685
So the audit question is this: do the property damage photos support the mechanism

00:02:12.685 --> 00:02:15.145
of injury described in the medical records?

00:02:15.495 --> 00:02:19.001
A full pre-demand audit is the final stress test of a file.

00:02:19.351 --> 00:02:22.288
It is the move from collecting information to weaponizing it.

00:02:24.838 --> 00:02:28.925
The Master Audit Checklist starts with financial and lien verification.

00:02:29.275 --> 00:02:31.202
This is the math portion of the audit.

00:02:31.552 --> 00:02:34.678
Special damages are the sum of all medical bills,

00:02:34.678 --> 00:02:37.102
plus property damage, plus lost wages.

00:02:37.452 --> 00:02:41.407
For lost wages, make sure you have a signed letter from the employer and a

00:02:41.407 --> 00:02:43.117
disability slip from the doctor.

00:02:43.467 --> 00:02:47.461
Without both, adjusters usually reject the lost wage claim.

00:02:47.811 --> 00:02:52.051
Then lien resolution: identify every subrogation interest,

00:02:52.051 --> 00:02:55.487
both statutory, like Medicare, Medicaid, ERISA,

00:02:55.487 --> 00:03:00.385
and the VA, and consensual, like medical provider liens under LOPs.

00:03:00.735 --> 00:03:05.042
Mistakes here lead to short-funded settlements where the client takes home nothing.

00:03:07.592 --> 00:03:09.984
Next on the checklist is liability proof.

00:03:10.334 --> 00:03:13.781
For the police or incident report, verify the final version,

00:03:13.781 --> 00:03:18.378
not just the exchange of information, and check it for citations or contributory

00:03:18.378 --> 00:03:19.355
negligence notes.

00:03:19.705 --> 00:03:23.777
For witness statements, confirm the contact information is still valid,

00:03:23.777 --> 00:03:27.332
and audit for statement drift: did the witness's story change?

00:03:27.682 --> 00:03:31.830
Then the scene audit: review Google Earth or dashcam footage to make sure the

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:33.661
physics of the claim are possible.

00:03:36.211 --> 00:03:39.056
Third, the property damage deep dive.

00:03:39.406 --> 00:03:41.914
Audit the photos for a clear point of impact.

00:03:42.264 --> 00:03:44.992
Are there photos of the undercarriage or the frame?

00:03:45.342 --> 00:03:48.024
Those are crucial in high-value injury claims.

00:03:48.374 --> 00:03:51.686
Compare the insurance estimate with the final body shop bill,

00:03:51.686 --> 00:03:55.108
and look for supplementals that prove hidden structural damage.

00:03:55.458 --> 00:04:00.208
And if the vehicle was totaled, verify the CCC One or other valuation report,

00:04:00.208 --> 00:04:04.896
and make sure the client is not being lowballed on the value of the vehicle.

00:04:07.446 --> 00:04:09.559
Fourth, the medical and billing audit.

00:04:09.909 --> 00:04:14.356
Build a chronology, a timeline of treatment, and audit it for gaps in treatment.

00:04:14.706 --> 00:04:18.277
If there is a 14-day gap, you must have a documented reason,

00:04:18.277 --> 00:04:21.730
for example, the client was waiting for MRI authorization.

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:27.084
Run a coding audit: scan the bills for unbundling or duplicate CPT codes that could

00:04:27.084 --> 00:04:30.822
make a bill look inflated, or even fraudulent, to an adjuster.

00:04:31.172 --> 00:04:35.923
And review the client's prior history, the past 5–10 years of medical records,

00:04:35.923 --> 00:04:39.822
so you find any pre-existing conditions before the defense does.

00:04:42.372 --> 00:04:46.167
Before finalizing the demand, audit the file the way the defense will,

00:04:46.167 --> 00:04:47.468
for these value killers.

00:04:47.818 --> 00:04:52.573
Social media presence: if the client claims loss of enjoyment of life but posted

00:04:52.573 --> 00:04:56.793
vacation photos during treatment, adjust the demand or warn the client.

00:04:57.143 --> 00:05:01.530
MIST designation: if the property damage is under one to two thousand dollars,

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:04.399
the case is classified as Minor Impact Soft Tissue,

00:05:04.399 --> 00:05:09.067
so provide stronger medical narratives to counter the bias that low damage means no

00:05:09.067 --> 00:05:09.461
injury.

00:05:09.811 --> 00:05:13.108
And the venue audit: confirm where a suit would be filed,

00:05:13.108 --> 00:05:16.174
and whether the jury pool is conservative or liberal,

00:05:16.174 --> 00:05:19.587
because that affects the multiplier for pain and suffering.

00:05:22.137 --> 00:05:26.293
An audited file is organized for the attorney's final review in three parts.

00:05:26.643 --> 00:05:30.161
A summary memo: a one-page cheat sheet of the audit findings.

00:05:30.511 --> 00:05:35.596
An exhibit index: all bills, records, and property damage photos, labeled.

00:05:35.946 --> 00:05:40.882
And the Ask: a calculated demand range, based on the policy limits and the total

00:05:40.882 --> 00:05:41.437
specials.

00:05:41.787 --> 00:05:45.549
A pro tip: always audit the policy limits one last time.

00:05:45.899 --> 00:05:49.590
There is nothing worse than sending a one hundred thousand dollar demand,

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:53.735
only to learn mid-negotiation that the defendant has a twenty-five thousand dollar

00:05:53.735 --> 00:05:54.595
step-down policy.

00:05:57.145 --> 00:05:59.211
Now we move into the Demand phase.

00:05:59.561 --> 00:06:03.380
Bodily Injury demand workflows bring together legal expertise,

00:06:03.380 --> 00:06:06.214
medical understanding, and negotiation skills.

00:06:06.564 --> 00:06:09.882
Case Managers must understand the why behind the paperwork,

00:06:09.882 --> 00:06:13.482
and that rests on three foundations: the Statute of Limitations,

00:06:13.482 --> 00:06:16.351
the liability framework, and the coverage analysis.

00:06:16.701 --> 00:06:20.777
Your job is to progress cases efficiently from intake to disbursement,

00:06:20.777 --> 00:06:24.329
meeting every deadline and identifying the key value drivers.

00:06:26.879 --> 00:06:31.741
In this phase, a Case Manager shifts from a reactive mindset to a proactive,

00:06:31.741 --> 00:06:32.637
strategic one.

00:06:32.987 --> 00:06:37.678
It is all about identifying needs, quantifying requirements, and setting the stage.

00:06:38.028 --> 00:06:40.454
Think in terms of the policy limit mindset.

00:06:40.804 --> 00:06:43.498
The goal is not just to send a packet of records.

00:06:43.848 --> 00:06:47.967
It is to present the case in a way that makes the insurance adjuster afraid of a

00:06:47.967 --> 00:06:48.225
jury.

00:06:48.575 --> 00:06:50.026
Two checks come first.

00:06:50.376 --> 00:06:55.286
Maximum Medical Improvement, or MMI: make sure the client has completed treatment,

00:06:55.286 --> 00:06:57.202
or has a clear future care plan.

00:06:57.552 --> 00:07:02.263
And the gap check: identify and explain any gaps in treatment before the adjuster

00:07:02.263 --> 00:07:04.008
uses them to devalue the case.

00:07:06.557 --> 00:07:08.694
Let's look closer at treatment gaps.

00:07:09.044 --> 00:07:12.455
Adjusters use them to argue that the injuries were not serious,

00:07:12.455 --> 00:07:15.054
that the treatment was unnecessary or excessive,

00:07:15.054 --> 00:07:17.598
that the client recovered earlier than claimed,

00:07:17.598 --> 00:07:20.468
or that other unrelated factors caused the condition.

00:07:20.818 --> 00:07:23.674
Unexplained gaps can reduce settlement value.

00:07:24.024 --> 00:07:28.313
So the Case Manager's strategy is to identify gaps early during record review,

00:07:28.313 --> 00:07:31.392
document the reason for every interruption in treatment,

00:07:31.392 --> 00:07:36.010
obtain supporting documents when available, and include the explanations directly in

00:07:36.010 --> 00:07:37.550
the demand package timeline.

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:41.274
When you address a gap in the demand, acknowledge it clearly,

00:07:41.274 --> 00:07:45.479
give the context and supporting facts, connect continued symptoms to ongoing

00:07:45.479 --> 00:07:49.684
treatment, and show the complaints were consistent before and after the gap.

00:07:50.034 --> 00:07:53.668
A well-explained treatment gap is less damaging than an unexplained one.

00:07:56.217 --> 00:08:00.566
Before the attorney reviews the demand letter, the Case Manager makes sure the Big

00:08:00.566 --> 00:08:02.104
Four components are flawless.

00:08:02.454 --> 00:08:06.426
Medical specials: a clean, itemized spreadsheet of every provider,

00:08:06.426 --> 00:08:08.352
date of service, and total bill.

00:08:08.702 --> 00:08:13.839
The narrative: drafting the human story, how the injury affected the client's job,

00:08:13.839 --> 00:08:15.969
their parenting, or their hobbies.

00:08:16.319 --> 00:08:19.660
Liability proof: the police report, witness statements,

00:08:19.660 --> 00:08:22.090
and clear photos of the property damage.

00:08:22.440 --> 00:08:27.882
And special damages: lost wage verifications, such as W-2s or employer letters,

00:08:27.882 --> 00:08:29.742
and out-of-pocket receipts.

00:08:32.292 --> 00:08:36.902
Before a demand is sent, the Case Manager performs a final scrub to catch the common

00:08:36.902 --> 00:08:37.726
adjuster traps.

00:08:38.076 --> 00:08:42.487
Billing overlaps: make sure there is no double billing for the same date of service.

00:08:42.837 --> 00:08:47.489
Pre-existing conditions: highlight how the accident aggravated a previous injury,

00:08:47.489 --> 00:08:49.211
rather than trying to hide it.

00:08:49.561 --> 00:08:53.925
And balance verification: call every provider to get the final balance,

00:08:53.925 --> 00:08:56.260
so the demand amount is 100% accurate.

00:08:58.810 --> 00:09:02.157
For senior Case Managers who handle the first call with the adjuster,

00:09:02.157 --> 00:09:04.000
there are two things you need to know.

00:09:04.350 --> 00:09:07.608
The anchor technique: never apologize for a high demand,

00:09:07.608 --> 00:09:09.644
and stay firm on the value drivers.

00:09:09.994 --> 00:09:13.594
And note-taking: document every excuse the adjuster gives,

00:09:13.594 --> 00:09:16.077
such as low impact or delayed treatment.

00:09:16.427 --> 00:09:20.712
Those notes are the ammunition the attorney needs if the case goes to litigation.

00:09:23.261 --> 00:09:25.653
Here are the best practices for the demand.

00:09:26.003 --> 00:09:31.260
The 30-day rule: once a client reaches MMI, the demand must be out the door within

00:09:31.260 --> 00:09:31.773
30 days.

00:09:32.123 --> 00:09:35.757
Photo impact: always lead the demand with the most graphic photo,

00:09:35.757 --> 00:09:38.776
of the property damage or the injury, to set the tone.

00:09:39.126 --> 00:09:43.381
The why factor: every medical bill in the demand needs a corresponding why,

00:09:43.381 --> 00:09:47.125
the reason that treatment was necessary for the client's recovery.

00:09:47.475 --> 00:09:50.923
And a pro tip to remember: a demand is not a request for money.

00:09:51.273 --> 00:09:53.166
It is a presentation of a debt owed.

00:09:53.516 --> 00:09:56.755
Treat the insurance company like a bank that has not paid its bill.

00:09:59.305 --> 00:10:01.894
Now we turn to settlement negotiations.

00:10:02.244 --> 00:10:05.692
In personal injury law, the demand letter is your opening move.

00:10:06.042 --> 00:10:09.316
If it looks like a template, the adjuster will treat it like one.

00:10:09.666 --> 00:10:11.756
Here is the negotiation battle map.

00:10:12.106 --> 00:10:16.075
First, create a net sheet: calculate the total medical liens,

00:10:16.075 --> 00:10:17.702
costs, and attorney fees.

00:10:18.052 --> 00:10:21.206
Anything below that total means the client receives nothing,

00:10:21.206 --> 00:10:23.624
and the aim is to maximize the net settlement.

00:10:23.974 --> 00:10:28.544
Second, identify the value drivers: list three facts that would scare a jury,

00:10:28.544 --> 00:10:33.470
such as the defendant was on his phone, or the client missed his daughter's wedding

00:10:33.470 --> 00:10:34.597
because of surgery.

00:10:34.947 --> 00:10:40.008
Third, anticipate the adjuster's response: name your top two weaknesses and prepare

00:10:40.008 --> 00:10:42.691
to justify them, ideally with documentation.

00:10:43.041 --> 00:10:47.627
And fourth, prepare the rebuttal, so the argument is ready before they bring it up.

00:10:47.977 --> 00:10:52.877
Adjusters protect insurance companies by undervaluing claims, so come prepared.

00:10:55.426 --> 00:10:57.667
Next, the valuation baseline.

00:10:58.017 --> 00:11:02.268
In Bodily Injury settlements, PIP benefits are paid regardless of fault,

00:11:02.268 --> 00:11:05.691
and that complicates the claim against the at-fault party.

00:11:06.041 --> 00:11:09.246
Case Managers need to understand how these coverages relate,

00:11:09.246 --> 00:11:11.916
to make sure the client does not end up with zero.

00:11:12.266 --> 00:11:17.392
In most jurisdictions, the defendant's insurance carrier is entitled to a set-off,

00:11:17.392 --> 00:11:19.580
a credit, for payments made by PIP.

00:11:19.930 --> 00:11:23.193
The logic is that the law generally prevents double recovery.

00:11:23.543 --> 00:11:26.379
If PIP already paid the hospital ten thousand dollars,

00:11:26.379 --> 00:11:30.265
the defendant should not have to pay that same ten thousand dollars again.

00:11:30.615 --> 00:11:33.696
So the calculation is: the gross settlement value,

00:11:33.696 --> 00:11:38.010
minus the PIP paid amount, equals the defendant's remaining liability.

00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:45.675
BI adjusters use PIP payments to gauge how serious a claim is during the Demand

00:11:45.675 --> 00:11:46.063
Phase.

00:11:46.413 --> 00:11:48.561
Look first at the speed of exhaustion.

00:11:48.911 --> 00:11:53.272
If a client exhausts a ten thousand dollar PIP limit in 48 hours,

00:11:53.272 --> 00:11:58.304
as John Doe did with his ER and EMS bills, it signals a high-severity case.

00:11:58.654 --> 00:12:00.325
Then look at special damages.

00:12:00.675 --> 00:12:05.563
The total medical bills, the specials, are usually much higher than what PIP pays.

00:12:05.913 --> 00:12:10.568
Even though PIP covered ten thousand dollars, the Case Manager argues that the total

00:12:10.568 --> 00:12:14.724
value of the bills, forty-one thousand four hundred dollars in John's case,

00:12:14.724 --> 00:12:17.883
is the true anchor for the pain and suffering multiplier.

00:12:20.433 --> 00:12:23.932
As we saw in John Doe's file with Doctor Sarah Spine,

00:12:23.932 --> 00:12:27.168
the Emergency Medical Condition finding, the EMC,

00:12:27.168 --> 00:12:29.082
is a major negotiation lever.

00:12:29.432 --> 00:12:33.809
Without an EMC, many states cap PIP at two thousand five hundred dollars.

00:12:34.159 --> 00:12:37.113
That pushes more of the bills onto the BI settlement,

00:12:37.113 --> 00:12:39.454
often eating up the client's net recovery.

00:12:39.804 --> 00:12:43.217
With an EMC, the full ten thousand dollars is available.

00:12:43.567 --> 00:12:47.851
That is better for negotiations, because it lowers the amount of unpaid medical

00:12:47.851 --> 00:12:51.160
liens that have to be negotiated down at the end of the case.

00:12:53.710 --> 00:12:57.539
This is the most critical technical detail in a BI negotiation:

00:12:57.539 --> 00:12:59.666
subrogation versus non-subrogation.

00:13:00.016 --> 00:13:02.789
Under non-subrogation, which is the common case,

00:13:02.789 --> 00:13:07.643
the PIP carrier cannot ask for its ten thousand dollars back from the BI settlement.

00:13:07.993 --> 00:13:11.652
That is a win for the client, because that money stays in their pocket,

00:13:11.652 --> 00:13:13.404
through the bills it already paid.

00:13:13.754 --> 00:13:18.374
Under subrogation, the PIP carrier places a lien on the BI settlement.

00:13:18.724 --> 00:13:23.243
The Case Manager must then negotiate with the PIP adjuster to reduce that lien,

00:13:23.243 --> 00:13:25.760
so the client keeps more of the final check.

00:13:28.310 --> 00:13:33.449
A savvy Case Manager often wants PIP exhausted as quickly as possible on the hard

00:13:33.449 --> 00:13:35.415
costs, like the ER and imaging.

00:13:35.765 --> 00:13:36.276
Why?

00:13:36.626 --> 00:13:41.021
Once PIP is exhausted, the client can move to Letters of Protection,

00:13:41.021 --> 00:13:45.740
or LOPs, for later treatment like chiropractic care and physical therapy.

00:13:46.090 --> 00:13:48.377
And that gives you negotiation leverage.

00:13:48.727 --> 00:13:53.852
LOP providers are often more willing to reduce their bills at the end of a case than

00:13:53.852 --> 00:13:54.706
a hospital is.

00:13:55.056 --> 00:13:58.472
By spending the PIP money on the non-negotiable hospital bills,

00:13:58.472 --> 00:14:01.291
you preserve the BI settlement funds for the client.

00:14:01.641 --> 00:14:06.935
One tip: always verify the PIP ledger before you begin BI negotiations.

00:14:07.285 --> 00:14:11.672
If you tell an adjuster the specials are forty thousand dollars but forget that PIP

00:14:11.672 --> 00:14:14.421
already paid ten thousand of that, your credibility,

00:14:14.421 --> 00:14:16.747
and your demand, will be cut down instantly.

00:14:19.297 --> 00:14:23.430
Negotiations officially begin once the demand packet is sent to the adjuster.

00:14:23.780 --> 00:14:26.044
Start with the soft lead, the first call.

00:14:26.394 --> 00:14:29.331
Confirm they received the demand and all the records.

00:14:29.681 --> 00:14:33.094
For example: Hi, I'm calling about the John Doe demand.

00:14:33.444 --> 00:14:37.659
I want to make sure you saw the MRI report on page 14 specifically.

00:14:38.009 --> 00:14:41.561
Then set the tone: state that the file is litigation ready.

00:14:41.911 --> 00:14:43.386
Next, the ask.

00:14:43.736 --> 00:14:48.068
If they have not made an offer, ask: Based on the clear liability and the forty

00:14:48.068 --> 00:14:51.468
thousand dollars in specials, what is your opening evaluation?

00:14:51.818 --> 00:14:54.384
And when the low-ball comes, never get angry.

00:14:54.734 --> 00:14:56.406
Use the professional pause.

00:14:56.756 --> 00:14:59.556
Count to five after they give a low number, then say:

00:14:59.556 --> 00:15:03.677
I'm struggling to see how that number accounts for the permanent nature of the

00:15:03.677 --> 00:15:04.047
injury.

00:15:04.397 --> 00:15:06.440
What data are you using to get there?

00:15:08.990 --> 00:15:12.776
When you are at one hundred thousand dollars and they are at thirty thousand,

00:15:12.776 --> 00:15:14.006
the gap feels impossible.

00:15:14.356 --> 00:15:15.807
Bracketing builds a bridge.

00:15:16.157 --> 00:15:20.104
First, propose the logic: We are seventy thousand dollars apart.

00:15:20.454 --> 00:15:23.395
If I can move my attorney into the eighty thousand range,

00:15:23.395 --> 00:15:25.563
can you get into the fifty thousand range?

00:15:25.913 --> 00:15:27.550
Second, check authority.

00:15:27.900 --> 00:15:31.382
If they say they don't have that much, ask: Who does?

00:15:31.732 --> 00:15:35.463
Because if we can't get into the fifties, we are wasting each other's time and

00:15:35.463 --> 00:15:36.945
should just file the complaint.

00:15:37.295 --> 00:15:38.828
Third, close the bracket.

00:15:39.178 --> 00:15:42.473
Once you are both between fifty and eighty thousand dollars,

00:15:42.473 --> 00:15:45.714
you negotiate the middle, for example, sixty-five thousand.

00:15:48.264 --> 00:15:52.472
Adjusters use stalls to wear you down, so you accept a lower end-of-month

00:15:52.472 --> 00:15:53.106
settlement.

00:15:53.456 --> 00:15:55.836
Here are three, with the rebuttal for each.

00:15:56.186 --> 00:15:59.227
The stall: I'm waiting on my manager's authority.

00:15:59.577 --> 00:16:01.214
The rebuttal: Understood.

00:16:01.564 --> 00:16:04.014
I'll give you until Thursday at 4 PM.

00:16:04.364 --> 00:16:08.458
If I don't hear back, I've been instructed to send the file to our litigation

00:16:08.458 --> 00:16:09.043
department.

00:16:09.393 --> 00:16:12.377
The stall: We need more records from five years ago.

00:16:12.727 --> 00:16:16.186
The rebuttal: Those aren't relevant to this acute injury.

00:16:16.536 --> 00:16:21.442
However, if you want them, I expect an in-good-faith offer on the current undisputed

00:16:21.442 --> 00:16:22.318
injuries today.

00:16:22.668 --> 00:16:26.221
And the stall: The property damage was too low for this injury.

00:16:26.571 --> 00:16:29.067
The rebuttal: Physics doesn't work that way.

00:16:29.417 --> 00:16:33.353
Low-velocity impacts often cause high-torque injuries to the spine,

00:16:33.353 --> 00:16:35.233
and we have the MRI to prove it.

00:16:37.783 --> 00:16:41.150
The last two thousand five hundred dollars is often the hardest to get.

00:16:41.500 --> 00:16:46.109
Use the split the baby move: We are at forty-seven thousand, you're at forty-three.

00:16:46.459 --> 00:16:50.662
Let's meet at forty-five thousand, and I'll get the release signed within the hour.

00:16:51.012 --> 00:16:53.497
Before you hang up, confirm the terms.

00:16:53.847 --> 00:16:55.785
Is this for Bodily Injury only?

00:16:56.135 --> 00:16:58.899
Save property damage for later if it is not settled.

00:16:59.249 --> 00:17:00.967
Does it include all liens?

00:17:01.317 --> 00:17:02.977
And when will the check be mailed?

00:17:03.327 --> 00:17:05.069
Then create the paper trail.

00:17:05.419 --> 00:17:08.475
Immediately email the adjuster: Per our conversation,

00:17:08.475 --> 00:17:12.396
we have settled the John Doe matter for forty-five thousand dollars.

00:17:12.746 --> 00:17:14.232
Please send the release.

00:17:16.782 --> 00:17:18.315
Finally, the never rule.

00:17:18.665 --> 00:17:20.847
Never apologize for a high demand.

00:17:21.197 --> 00:17:23.275
It is based on the client's suffering.

00:17:23.625 --> 00:17:25.135
Never bid against yourself.

00:17:25.485 --> 00:17:29.711
If you drop your price, the adjuster must raise theirs before you move again.

00:17:30.061 --> 00:17:33.648
And never accept that's all I have as the final answer on the first day.

00:17:33.998 --> 00:17:36.994
Every adjuster has a supervisor with a bigger checkbook.

00:17:39.543 --> 00:17:43.920
Now we reach the BI Settlement phase, and the UM/UIM safety check.

00:17:44.270 --> 00:17:47.228
If the at-fault driver has low or no insurance,

00:17:47.228 --> 00:17:52.200
you must tap into the client's own Uninsured or Underinsured Motorist coverage.

00:17:52.550 --> 00:17:55.162
That makes the subrogation waiver your green light.

00:17:55.512 --> 00:17:59.748
If you settle with the at-fault driver and sign their release without a waiver from

00:17:59.748 --> 00:18:03.830
your client's own insurer, you may destroy your client's right to collect UM/UIM

00:18:03.830 --> 00:18:04.289
benefits.

00:18:04.639 --> 00:18:09.555
So, first, send the 30-day letter: a formal notice to the UM carrier about the

00:18:09.555 --> 00:18:09.934
offer.

00:18:10.283 --> 00:18:14.569
Then secure the waiver document: a written Waiver of Subrogation from the UM

00:18:14.569 --> 00:18:15.020
carrier.

00:18:15.370 --> 00:18:18.523
It proves they have given up their right to sue the driver,

00:18:18.523 --> 00:18:22.371
which lets you settle the liability claim and proceed with the UM claim.

00:18:24.921 --> 00:18:28.653
Before a settlement can be discussed, the file must be bulletproof,

00:18:28.653 --> 00:18:31.272
and that rests on three key laws and doctrines.

00:18:31.622 --> 00:18:35.366
The Made Whole Doctrine says a health insurer, the subrogated carrier,

00:18:35.366 --> 00:18:39.110
cannot claim from a settlement unless the client is fully compensated.

00:18:39.460 --> 00:18:44.313
It can help persuade lienholders to waive their claims when the settlement is small

00:18:44.313 --> 00:18:46.008
next to significant injuries.

00:18:46.358 --> 00:18:51.748
The Common Fund Doctrine requires lienholders to reduce their bill by the attorney's

00:18:51.748 --> 00:18:53.672
fee percentage, usually 33.3%.

00:18:54.022 --> 00:18:58.296
The logic: they should not get a free ride on the work the firm did to recover the

00:18:58.296 --> 00:18:58.608
money.

00:18:58.958 --> 00:19:02.325
And the Statute of Limitations is the death date for a case.

00:19:02.675 --> 00:19:07.010
Case Managers must track it religiously, because missing it is an automatic

00:19:07.010 --> 00:19:08.051
malpractice event.

00:19:10.601 --> 00:19:11.924
Now the execution.

00:19:12.274 --> 00:19:14.899
The Release of All Claims is the exit document,

00:19:14.899 --> 00:19:17.301
and the most critical document in the file.

00:19:17.651 --> 00:19:20.484
It is the contract that legally ends the dispute.

00:19:20.834 --> 00:19:25.766
In a release of Bodily Injury, make sure the client is not accidentally signing away

00:19:25.766 --> 00:19:30.052
a property damage or PIP claim that is still being negotiated separately.

00:19:30.402 --> 00:19:35.011
Know the non-admission of liability: standard releases state the insurer is paying

00:19:35.011 --> 00:19:39.226
to avoid further litigation, not because it admits its driver was at fault.

00:19:39.576 --> 00:19:42.386
And know the indemnity and hold harmless clause:

00:19:42.386 --> 00:19:45.312
the claimant, and their Case Manager and attorney,

00:19:45.312 --> 00:19:47.947
are responsible for paying all medical liens.

00:19:48.297 --> 00:19:51.850
If a hospital later sues the insurer over an unpaid bill,

00:19:51.850 --> 00:19:56.215
the claimant must indemnify, or pay back, the insurer for those costs.

00:19:58.765 --> 00:20:01.795
The Settlement Disclosure Statement is the truth document.

00:20:02.145 --> 00:20:05.364
It is often required by state law or internal compliance,

00:20:05.364 --> 00:20:09.262
and it makes sure the claimant understands exactly what is happening.

00:20:09.612 --> 00:20:12.139
It states that the settlement is full and final:

00:20:12.139 --> 00:20:15.245
even if the claimant's back starts hurting again next year,

00:20:15.245 --> 00:20:17.193
they cannot come back for more money.

00:20:17.543 --> 00:20:21.815
And some states have a cooling-off period, a rescission period of a few business

00:20:21.815 --> 00:20:25.020
days, when the claimant can change their mind after signing.

00:20:25.370 --> 00:20:28.713
The Case Manager must track that date before cutting any checks.

00:20:31.263 --> 00:20:34.061
Lien payoff letters are the verification paperwork.

00:20:34.411 --> 00:20:37.395
A Case Manager cannot rely on a medical bill alone.

00:20:37.745 --> 00:20:40.450
You need a formal final demand, or payoff letter.

00:20:40.800 --> 00:20:46.911
If the claimant is 65+ or on SSDI, CMS, the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid

00:20:46.911 --> 00:20:50.483
Services, will issue a Medicare final demand.

00:20:50.833 --> 00:20:56.493
Be warned: it can take 60+ days to arrive, and the file must stay pended until it is

00:20:56.493 --> 00:20:57.033
in hand.

00:20:57.383 --> 00:21:00.625
And once a provider, like a chiropractor or a surgeon,

00:21:00.625 --> 00:21:04.349
is paid, obtain a signed letter stating the lien is satisfied.

00:21:04.699 --> 00:21:09.138
That satisfaction of lien keeps the provider from sending the client to collections

00:21:09.138 --> 00:21:09.459
later.

00:21:12.009 --> 00:21:16.286
The Settlement Statement is the net sheet: the ledger that breaks the settlement

00:21:16.286 --> 00:21:20.136
down from gross to net, and as a Case Manager you audit it for accuracy.

00:21:20.485 --> 00:21:22.378
Start with cost verification.

00:21:22.728 --> 00:21:27.318
Make sure every case cost, such as fifty dollars for a police report or two hundred

00:21:27.318 --> 00:21:30.193
dollars for medical records, is backed by a receipt.

00:21:30.543 --> 00:21:34.920
Then check the net to client, the final number the client actually receives.

00:21:35.270 --> 00:21:39.542
Your goal is to make sure that number is enough to cover any future medical needs

00:21:39.542 --> 00:21:41.493
discussed during the treatment phase.

00:21:44.043 --> 00:21:45.877
The dismissal is the court filing.

00:21:46.227 --> 00:21:50.078
If the case was already in litigation, meaning a lawsuit was filed,

00:21:50.078 --> 00:21:53.240
the settlement is not over until the court is notified.

00:21:53.590 --> 00:21:57.595
A dismissal with prejudice means the case is closed and cannot be refiled.

00:21:57.945 --> 00:22:01.356
A dismissal without prejudice, which is rare in settlements,

00:22:01.356 --> 00:22:04.540
allows the case to be refiled under specific conditions.

00:22:04.890 --> 00:22:08.825
So the Case Manager verifies that with prejudice is the language used,

00:22:08.825 --> 00:22:10.962
to make sure the file is fully closed.

00:22:13.511 --> 00:22:18.297
Many insurance carriers will not issue a settlement check until they have a W-9 form

00:22:18.297 --> 00:22:20.234
from the law firm or the claimant.

00:22:20.584 --> 00:22:24.485
If the Case Manager does not request it the moment the verbal agreement is made,

00:22:24.485 --> 00:22:26.679
the check will be delayed by at least a week.

00:22:27.029 --> 00:22:29.908
Here is how the key post-settlement documents compare.

00:22:30.258 --> 00:22:33.637
The release is signed by the claimant, and it ends the right to sue.

00:22:33.987 --> 00:22:37.935
The final demand comes from the lienholder, such as Medicare or a hospital,

00:22:37.935 --> 00:22:40.883
and it states the exact amount needed to clear the debt.

00:22:41.233 --> 00:22:44.955
The W-9 is signed by the payee, the law firm or the claimant,

00:22:44.955 --> 00:22:46.724
for tax reporting to the IRS.

00:22:47.074 --> 00:22:50.143
And the stipulation to dismiss is signed by the attorneys,

00:22:50.143 --> 00:22:52.682
and it notifies the court that the case is over.

00:22:55.232 --> 00:22:58.870
Once the settlement is finalized and the paperwork is understood,

00:22:58.870 --> 00:23:01.780
the Case Manager enters the technical closing stage.

00:23:02.130 --> 00:23:05.643
Your mission is to move the file from settled to paid and archived,

00:23:05.643 --> 00:23:06.797
with total compliance.

00:23:07.147 --> 00:23:11.064
These are the boots-on-the-ground tasks, starting with administrative document

00:23:11.064 --> 00:23:11.466
control.

00:23:11.816 --> 00:23:16.111
The signature chase: send the Release of All Claims to the claimant by secure

00:23:16.111 --> 00:23:19.513
e-signature, like DocuSign, or schedule an in-person signing.

00:23:19.863 --> 00:23:22.763
Notary coordination: if the release needs a notary,

00:23:22.763 --> 00:23:27.481
which is common for high-value BI claims, verify that the notary's stamp is current

00:23:27.481 --> 00:23:29.755
and the ID matches the claimant exactly.

00:23:30.105 --> 00:23:34.523
And W-9 procurement: collect a W-9 from the claimant or the firm right away,

00:23:34.523 --> 00:23:38.302
so the carrier's accounting department does not flag the payment.

00:23:40.852 --> 00:23:44.973
The second set of closing tasks is final lien mitigation, the reduction.

00:23:45.323 --> 00:23:49.352
For provider negotiation, call the billing department of every lienholder.

00:23:49.702 --> 00:23:52.523
The script: The settlement was lower than expected.

00:23:52.873 --> 00:23:58.019
In the interest of global resolution, will you accept 50% of the balance as payment

00:23:58.019 --> 00:23:58.515
in full?

00:23:58.865 --> 00:24:00.317
Then update the ledger.

00:24:00.667 --> 00:24:04.396
Reflect every saving or reduction in the Settlement Statement right away,

00:24:04.396 --> 00:24:07.052
so the client can see their net recovery increasing.

00:24:07.402 --> 00:24:11.360
And for Medicare and Medicaid finalization, log into the MSPRP,

00:24:11.360 --> 00:24:16.636
the Medicare Secondary Payer Recovery Portal, and make sure the final demand matches

00:24:16.636 --> 00:24:17.956
your internal ledger.

00:24:20.506 --> 00:24:22.630
That brings us to the end of Day 2.

00:24:22.980 --> 00:24:27.832
Today we audited the file before the demand, built and presented the Bodily Injury

00:24:27.832 --> 00:24:30.731
demand, and negotiated and closed the settlement,

00:24:30.731 --> 00:24:33.452
all the way from settled to paid and archived.

00:24:33.802 --> 00:24:38.444
Review your notes on John Doe's file tonight, because we build on every one of these

00:24:38.444 --> 00:24:39.770
steps in the days ahead.

00:24:40.120 --> 00:24:40.782
Thank you.

